= What Biodiversity Really Means

Walk from a city park into a tropical forest and the world changes fast. More kinds of trees, more insects, more birds — and the variety itself has a name. The biologist Edward Wilson popularised the word *biodiversity* for the combined diversity — the total variety of life — at all levels of biological organisation, from genes up to whole ecosystems. That word packs three levels into one idea, and every exam question starts by asking which level is being discussed. So let us meet the levels one by one, with everyday examples.

- *Genetic diversity — variety inside one species:* members of the same species carry slightly different gene versions, like students of one class with different handwriting. India has more than *50,000 genetically different strains of rice* and about *1,000 varieties of mango* — all one species, many gene combinations.
- *Species diversity — variety of species in a place:* this is the level you picture first. The *Western Ghats* hold a greater amphibian species diversity (more kinds of frogs and their relatives) than the *Eastern Ghats*.
- *Ecological diversity — variety of ecosystems in a region:* an ecosystem is a community of organisms plus the physical setting they live in. India spans *deserts, rain forests, mangroves, coral reefs, wetlands, estuaries and alpine meadows* — a far greater ecosystem variety than a Scandinavian country like *Norway*.

