== How much life have we actually counted?

- *Counted so far:* about *1.7–1.8 million species* of plants and animals have been described — formally named, checked and catalogued.
- *The real number is far higher:* the ecologist *Robert May* places global species diversity at about *7 million* — so most species on Earth are still unnamed.
- *Insects rule the count:* among animals, insects make up *more than 70 per cent* of all species — 7 out of every 10 animals on Earth is an insect.
- *A fungi surprise:* the number of fungi species in the world is *more than* the combined total of fishes, amphibians, reptiles and mammals.
- *Why counting matters:* you cannot protect what you have not named. Species lists are the base of every conservation plan.

#analogy-box(title: "Analogy — The Library of Life")[
  Picture Earth as one giant library. Each *word* is a gene — a small unit that means little alone but combines into sense. Each finished *book* is a species — one complete working set of words. Each *shelf* is an ecosystem — books arranged, interacting, in their setting. The mapping is exact: genes = words, species = books, ecosystems = shelves. Now the hard part: we have catalogued only a fraction of the books, and we are burning shelves faster than we can read them. Most fires destroy books nobody has opened yet — species going extinct before anyone names them.
]

== Which level is which? A 60-second drill

Questions rarely say "genetic diversity" directly. They give you an example and expect you to name the level. Practise the translation now.

#worked-example("Worked Example — Name the Level")[
  - *"India has more than 50,000 genetically different strains of rice."*
    All strains are one species, so the variety sits *within* the species → *genetic diversity*.
  - *"The Western Ghats have a greater amphibian diversity than the Eastern Ghats."*
    Different frog species are being counted → *species diversity*.
  - *"India has deserts, rain forests, mangroves, coral reefs and alpine meadows."*
    Different ecosystems, not different genes or species → *ecological diversity*.

  The sorting rule: variety *within* one species = genetic; variety *among* species = species; variety *of habitats and ecosystems* = ecological.
]

- *India punches above its weight:* with only *2.4 per cent* of the world's land area, India holds about *8.1 per cent* of the world's species — a "mega-biodiversity" country in official listings.
- *Keep the three numbers together:* ~1.7–1.8 million described, ~7 million estimated, 70 per cent insects. They anchor many one-mark checks.

= Patterns in Biodiversity

Where on Earth would you find the most species? The answer is remarkably tidy: richness climbs towards the equator and falls towards the poles, and within any region it grows with the area you explore. Two patterns — the *latitudinal gradient* and the *species–area relationship* — carry almost every question from this part of the unit.

== The latitudinal gradient: the tropics win

*Latitudinal gradient* means the change in species richness as you move from the poles (high latitudes) to the equator (low latitudes). The tropics — the belt between *23.5° N* and *23.5° S* — harbour more species than temperate or polar zones.

- *The bird numbers tell the story:* *Colombia*, near the equator, has nearly *1,400 bird species*; *New York* at 41° N has *105*; *Greenland* at 71° N has only *56*.
- *India sits in the rich belt:* with much of its land in the tropics, India has *more than 1,200 bird species*.
- *Plants follow the same slope:* a tropical forest in Ecuador can hold up to *10 times* as many vascular plant species as an equal-area temperate forest in the Midwestern USA.
- *The champion region:* the *Amazonian rain forest* of South America carries the *greatest biodiversity on Earth*.

