== Why the tropics are so rich — the three reasons

NCERT gives exactly three reasons; learn them as a fixed trio.

- *Long evolutionary time:* temperate zones were hit by repeated *glaciations* (ice ages) that reset life; the tropics stayed *relatively undisturbed for millions of years*, so species had a long run to diversify.
- *Constant climate:* tropical environments are *less seasonal and more predictable*, so species can specialise tightly into narrow niches — a *niche* is a species' job and address in its ecosystem.
- *More solar energy:* the tropics receive *more sunlight*, giving higher productivity (more plant growth), which supports more species at every level.

== The species–area relationship

Count a small patch of forest, then a bigger one: the bigger patch holds more species — but the rise flattens out. The German naturalist *Alexander von Humboldt* noticed that richness grows with explored area *only up to a limit*, and the pattern fits a neat power equation.

#formula-box(title: "Species–Area Relationship")[
  $S = C dot A^z$
  #v(6pt)
  $"log" S = "log" C + z "log" A$
  #v(8pt)
  $S$ = number of species
  #v(2pt)
  $A$ = area explored
  #v(2pt)
  $C$ = constant (intercept — species at unit area)
  #v(2pt)
  $z$ = slope (how fast richness rises with area)
]

The second form is just the same equation with logarithms (log = the power to which 10 must be raised), which turns the curve into a straight line — that is the line whose slope is $z$.

- *Small and medium regions:* whether you count plants in Britain, birds in California or molluscs in New York State, the slope $z$ falls in a narrow *0.1 to 0.2* range.
- *Very large regions:* for areas the size of *whole continents*, $z$ steepens to *0.6 to 1.2*. For fruit-eating birds and mammals across the tropical forests of different continents, $z$ is about *1.1*.
- *Why z matters:* a steeper slope says richness depends strongly on area — so destroying habitat there wipes out species much faster.

#worked-example("Worked Example — The Equation at Work")[
  A forest study fits the equation with *C = 10* and *z = 0.2*. Note that $0.2 = 1/5$, so $A^0.2$ is simply the *fifth root* of the area.

  - *Patch A — 100 km²:*
    $S = 10 times 100^0.2$
    #v(2pt)
    $100^0.2 approx 2.512$
    #v(2pt)
    $S approx 10 times 2.512 approx 25$ species
  - *Patch B — 400 km² (four times the area):*
    $S = 10 times 400^0.2$
    #v(2pt)
    $400^0.2 approx 3.314$
    #v(2pt)
    $S approx 10 times 3.314 approx 33$ species
  - *Read the lesson:* four times the area lifted richness only from 25 to 33. Richness grows gently with area — and the reverse also holds gently at first, which is why the *last* fragments of a destroyed forest matter so much: the curve steepens as regions grow large ($z$ up to 1.2).
]

- *Bridge to conservation:* the same equation lets biologists *predict* how many species a shrinking habitat will lose — the mathematical heart of the habitat-loss story you will meet in a later section.

= Why Biodiversity Matters — Three Views

Why should a farmer, a doctor and a monk all care about a beetle? Because each looks at nature through a different window, and every window is a valid answer to "why conserve?". The syllabus groups the arguments into three views — *narrowly utilitarian*, *broadly utilitarian* and *ethical*. Learn the labels, because matching a statement to its view is a classic question type.

== Narrowly utilitarian — biodiversity pays the bills

*Narrowly utilitarian* means valuing nature for the direct products humans extract and sell or use.

- *Food, fibre and fuel:* crops, fruits, meat, wool, cotton, firewood — the oldest "services" biodiversity offers.
- *Medicines:* about *25 per cent* of the drugs sold worldwide contain plant extracts or active ingredients derived from plants.
- *Bioprospecting:* exploring molecular, genetic and species-level diversity to find products of economic importance — nature treated as a research mine.

== Broadly utilitarian — nature works for free

*Broadly utilitarian* means valuing the *ecosystem services* — the unpaid work nature does for everyone, whether or not anyone pays for it.

- *Oxygen:* the Amazonian rain forest is called the *"lungs of the planet"* — it contributes about *20 per cent* of the oxygen in Earth's atmosphere through photosynthesis.
- *Pollination:* bees, bumblebees, birds and bats pollinate crops; without them there would be few fruits and seeds.
- *Climate and flood control:* forests moderate rainfall, hold soil and soften floods.
- *The price tag:* in 1997, *Robert Constanza* and colleagues valued nature's life-support services at about *US \$33 trillion a year* — nearly twice the global GNP (US \$18 trillion).
- *NCERT's key point:* a single species' product value can be tiny compared with its *service* value — a bee costs almost nothing in the market, yet its pollination work keeps farms alive.

== Ethical — every species has intrinsic value

*Ethical* means valuing species for their own sake — the philosophical window.

- *Intrinsic value:* every species has worth *in itself*, even if it earns humans nothing today.
- *A duty to the future:* we should pass biological wealth on to coming generations in as good a shape as we received it.
- *People already act on it:* the *Bishnoi* community of Rajasthan protects trees and wild animals as a religious duty.

