    [Listing the Nile perch under over-exploitation],
    [The Nile perch episode is *alien species invasion* — the fish was introduced into Lake Victoria.],
    [A fish dying looks like fishing; the species was never hunted, it *arrived* and outcompeted natives.],
    [Treating sacred groves as ex situ, or as tourist sites],
    [Sacred groves are *in situ* — the forest is protected exactly where it stands.],
    ["Religious" makes students think of people, not habitat; nothing is ever moved.],
    [Writing co-extinction for any species that dies out],
    [Co-extinction needs an *obligate partner* — a host–parasite or plant–pollinator pair.],
    [The word only applies when a second species is dragged down by the first.],
    [Applying $S = C dot A^z$ without the log form],
    [The slope $z$ comes from the straight-line log form: $"log" S = "log" C + z "log" A$.],
    [The power equation is a curve; only its logarithm draws the line whose slope is $z$.],
    [Assuming ex situ means releasing animals into the wild],
    [Ex situ keeps the species *away* from its natural home, under human care; release is a later step, not the method.],
    [Students picture freedom; the defining feature of ex situ is exactly the opposite.],
  )
]

- *Three checking habits:* attach every named example to its driver before reading the options; tick *both* hotspot criteria, never one; and match each number (−196 °C, 25%, 0.1–0.2, 0.6–1.2) to its term the night before the exam.

