= Practice Questions

#mcq(1, [The word *biodiversity* — the combined diversity at all levels of biological organisation — was popularised by:], (
  [Robert May], [Robert Constanza], [Alexander von Humboldt], [Edward Wilson],
))

#mcq(2, [India has more than 50,000 genetically different strains of rice and about 1,000 varieties of mango. This variety *within* a single species is an example of:], (
  [genetic diversity], [species diversity], [ecological diversity], [habitat fragmentation],
))

#mcq(3, [In the species–area relationship $S = C dot A^z$, a region has $C = 10$ and $z = 0.2$. For an area of 10,000 km², the predicted number of species is closest to:], (
  [20], [63], [100], [200],
))

#mcq(4, [Colombia near the equator holds nearly 1,400 bird species, while Greenland at 71° N holds only 56. The steady fall in richness from the tropics towards the poles is called the:], (
  [species–area relationship], [niche specialisation], [latitudinal gradient], [habitat fragmentation],
))

#mcq(5, [Exploring molecular, genetic and species-level diversity to find products of economic importance — for example, new medicines from plants — is called:], (
  [biopiracy], [bioremediation], [biomass estimation], [bioprospecting],
))

#mcq(6, [For very large areas such as entire continents, the slope $z$ of the species–area relationship is found to lie close to:], (
  [0.1 to 0.2], [0.2 to 0.4], [0.6 to 1.2], [1.5 to 2.0],
))

#mcq(7, [The Nile perch, introduced into Lake Victoria, wiped out more than 200 species of cichlid fish. This loss is best classified as caused by:], (
  [over-exploitation], [alien species invasion], [habitat fragmentation], [co-extinction],
))

#mcq(8, [Steller's sea cow and the passenger pigeon disappeared soon after humans began hunting them heavily. The driver of their extinction was:], (
  [alien species invasion], [co-extinction], [over-exploitation], [habitat fragmentation],
))

#mcq(9, [A region qualifies as a biodiversity hotspot only when it has:], (
  [very high endemism together with very high habitat loss], [the largest number of national parks in the country], [the world's largest area of tropical forest], [a government ban on all hunting and fishing],
))

#mcq(10, [Gametes of a threatened animal are kept viable and fertile for long periods in liquid nitrogen at −196 °C. This ex situ technique is called:], (
  [tissue culture], [cryopreservation], [in situ conservation], [deforestation control],
))

= Solutions

#solution(1, [*Biodiversity* as the combined diversity at all levels of biological organisation was popularised by the biologist *Edward Wilson*. Robert May estimated ~7 million species; Robert Constanza priced ecosystem services; Alexander von Humboldt gave the species–area idea.], answer: "D")

#solution(2, [All rice strains and mango varieties belong to *one species*; the differences lie in gene versions *within* that species → *genetic diversity*. Species diversity would need many different species, ecological diversity many different ecosystems.], answer: "A")

#solution(3, [Since $0.2 = 1/5$, the factor $10000^0.2$ is the fifth root of 10,000, which is *6.31*. Then $S = 10 times 6.31 approx 63$. (Check: $6.31^5 approx 10,000$.)], answer: "B")

#solution(4, [Richness falling steadily from equator to pole — Colombia ~1,400, New York 105, Greenland 56 — is the *latitudinal gradient*: the tropics (23.5° N to 23.5° S) hold the most species. The species–area relationship instead links richness to *area*, not latitude.], answer: "C")

#solution(5, [Systematically searching biodiversity at the molecular, genetic and species level for economically useful products is *bioprospecting*. Bioremediation is cleanup with organisms; biopiracy is unauthorised commercial use of traditional knowledge.], answer: "D")

#solution(6, [For small and medium regions $z$ sits at 0.1–0.2, but for *very large areas* — whole continents — the slope steepens to *0.6 to 1.2*; for fruit-eating birds and mammals across tropical forests of different continents it is about 1.1.], answer: "C")

#solution(7, [The Nile perch was *introduced* into Lake Victoria and then outcompeted the native cichlids — no hunting of cichlids, no removal of their habitat. An introduced species turning invasive = *alien species invasion*.], answer: "B")

#solution(8, [Both species were abundant until humans harvested them faster than they could breed — the definition of *over-exploitation*. No new species arrived and no obligate partner was involved.], answer: "C")

#solution(9, [The hotspot label carries *both* official criteria: very high species richness with very high *endemism*, *plus* very high habitat loss. Park counts, forest size or legal bans are not part of the definition.], answer: "A")

#solution(10, [Freezing gametes in liquid nitrogen at *−196 °C* so they stay viable and fertile for long periods is *cryopreservation* — an ex situ method, because the material is preserved away from the natural setting. Tissue culture regrows plants; in situ protects species in the wild.], answer: "B")

